{
  "id": "ep028",
  "duration": 56.651,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.972,
      "text": "The most famous star in the sky ranks 48th in brightness.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "A Colorful Night (iotw2025a).tiff",
        "credit": "KPNO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/B. Tafreshi",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Colorful_Night_(iotw2025a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 3.967,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.972,
      "end": 9.442,
      "text": "Millions of skywatchers mistakenly believe Polaris is the brightest star above Earth.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Location of the EGS Field on the Sky (opo0706f).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/ESA, The Hubble Heritage Team and A. Riess (STScI)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_of_the_EGS_Field_on_the_Sky_(opo0706f).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 3.967,
          "end": 6.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 6.033,
          "end": 9.433,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 9.442,
      "end": 16.887,
      "text": "In reality, Sirius blazes roughly 25 times brighter because it sits only 8.7 light-years away.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The Dog Star, Sirius, and its Tiny Companion (2005-36-1820).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, H.E. Bond and E. Nelan (Space Telescope Science Institute, Baltimore, Md.); M. Barstow and M. Burleigh (University of Leicester, U.K.); and J.B. Holberg (University of Arizona)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Dog_Star,_Sirius,_and_its_Tiny_Companion_(2005-36-1820).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 9.433,
          "end": 12.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 12.6,
          "end": 16.9,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 16.887,
      "end": 24.482,
      "text": "Polaris sits over 430 light-years away, though it is intrinsically thousands of times more luminous than our Sun.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Polaris A and Polaris B (opo0602c).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, N. Evans (Harvard-Smithsonian CfA), and H. Bond (STScI)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polaris_A_and_Polaris_B_(opo0602c).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 16.9,
          "end": 24.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 24.482,
      "end": 32.227,
      "text": "Navigators rely on Polaris solely because it aligns almost directly above Earth's northern rotational axis.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Big Dipper (ann22042ah).jpg",
        "credit": "Arya Anthony/IAU OAE",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Big_Dipper_(ann22042ah).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 24.467,
          "end": 28.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 28.333,
          "end": 32.233,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 32.227,
      "end": 38.722,
      "text": "But Earth wobbles on its rotational axis over a 26,000-year cycle called precession.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 32.233,
          "end": 38.733,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 38.722,
      "end": 45.992,
      "text": "Around the year 2100, Polaris reaches its closest northern alignment before slowly drifting away.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Polaris system.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/ESA/HST, G. Bacon (STScI)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polaris_system.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 38.733,
          "end": 42.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 42.033,
          "end": 46.0,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 45.992,
      "end": 51.767,
      "text": "In 13,000 years, the brilliant star Vega will take over as our new North Star.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Vega by Stephen Rahn.jpg",
        "credit": "Stephen Rahn",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vega_by_Stephen_Rahn.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 46.0,
          "end": 48.9,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 48.9,
          "end": 51.767,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 51.767,
      "end": 56.301,
      "text": "Until then, many will still falsely assume that the North Star is the brightest.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "A Colorful Night (iotw2025a).tiff",
        "credit": "KPNO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/B. Tafreshi",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Colorful_Night_(iotw2025a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 51.767,
          "end": 56.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
